# 🌊 LeetCode 75 — Day 11: Container With Most Water

**📌 Problem:** [11. Container With Most Water](https://leetcode.com/problems/container-with-most-water/)
**🏷️ Topics:** Array, Two Pointers, Greedy
**⚡ Difficulty:** Medium

---

## 🧠 Intuition

The area between two lines is `width × min(height of the two lines)`. Starting with the widest possible container (the two outer edges) gives the maximum possible width right away. From there, moving the **shorter** of the two pointers inward is the only move that can possibly increase the area — moving the taller one inward can only shrink the width while the height stays capped by the shorter line anyway, so it can never help.

---

## 🛠️ Approach

1. Set two pointers: `lp = 0` (left edge) and `rp = height.size() - 1` (right edge).
2. While `lp < rp`:
   - Compute the current width `w = rp - lp` and the limiting height `ht = min(height[lp], height[rp])`.
   - Compute `currWater = w * ht` and update `maxWater` if it's larger.
   - Move whichever pointer points to the **shorter** line inward (`lp++` if `height[lp] < height[rp]`, else `rp--`).
3. Return `maxWater` once the pointers meet.

---

## ⏱️ Complexity

- **Time complexity:** `O(n)` — each pointer moves inward at most `n` times total, so the array is traversed once.
- **Space complexity:** `O(1)` — only a handful of integer variables are used.

---

## 💻 Code

**Language/Format:** C++ — solved using LeetCode's OOP submission format (`class Solution` with a public method), using a converging two-pointer technique with `min()`/`max()` from the standard library.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxArea(vector<int>& height) {
        int maxWater = 0;
        int lp = 0, rp = height.size() - 1;

        while (lp < rp) {
            int w = rp - lp;
            int ht = min(height[lp], height[rp]);
            int currWater = w * ht;

            maxWater = max(maxWater, currWater);
            height[lp] < height[rp] ? lp++ : rp--;
        }
        return maxWater;
    }
};
```

---

## 🔑 Key Takeaway

Watch out for `size() - 1` when initializing a right-hand pointer to the last valid index — using `size()` directly reads one past the end of the array, which is undefined behavior in C++ and can silently pull garbage values into your calculations instead of throwing a clean error.

